{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial02","statement":"The poll app defines two models: Question and Choice.","source_span":"we'll create two models: ``Question`` and ``Choice``"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial02","statement":"The Question model has fields question_text (CharField, max_length=200) and pub_date (DateTimeField, \"date published\").","source_span":"question_text = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n        pub_date = models.DateTimeField(\"date published\")"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial02","statement":"The Choice model has fields question (ForeignKey to Question, on_delete=models.CASCADE), choice_text (CharField, max_length=200), and votes (IntegerField, default=0).","source_span":"question = models.ForeignKey(Question, on_delete=models.CASCADE)\n        choice_text = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n        votes = models.IntegerField(default=0)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial02","statement":"Django supports many-to-one, many-to-many, and one-to-one database relationships.","source_span":"Django supports all the common database relationships: many-to-one, many-to-many, and one-to-one."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial02","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What database does Django's mysite/settings.py use by default, and why is it convenient for beginners?","answer":"SQLite. It's convenient because SQLite is included in Python, so no additional installation is needed to support the database."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial02","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What command creates the database tables for the apps listed in INSTALLED_APPS, and what does it look at to do so?","answer":"The command `python manage.py migrate`; it looks at the INSTALLED_APPS setting and creates any necessary database tables according to your database settings."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial02","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Which six applications does Django include by default in INSTALLED_APPS?","answer":"django.contrib.admin, django.contrib.auth, django.contrib.contenttypes, django.contrib.sessions, django.contrib.messages, and django.contrib.staticfiles."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial02","fact_refs":[6,7,8],"question":"What are the two models created for the poll app in this tutorial, and what fields does each contain?","answer":"Question, with fields question_text (CharField, max_length=200) and pub_date (DateTimeField); and Choice, with fields question (ForeignKey to Question, on_delete=CASCADE), choice_text (CharField, max_length=200), and votes (IntegerField, default=0)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial02","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What does the django.contrib.admin app, included by default in Django, provide?","answer":"The admin site."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial02","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What three types of database relationships does Django support between models?","answer":"Many-to-one, many-to-many, and one-to-one relationships."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial03","statement":"Tutorial 3 focuses on creating the public interface, called \"views,\" for the poll application.","source_span":"will focus on creating the public\ninterface -- \"views.\""}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial03","statement":"The poll application has four views: a question index page, a question detail page, a question results page, and a vote action.","source_span":"we'll have the following four views:"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial03","statement":"polls/urls.py maps the URL patterns \"\" to views.index (name=\"index\"), \"<int:question_id>/\" to views.detail (name=\"detail\"), \"<int:question_id>/results/\" to views.results (name=\"results\"), and \"<int:question_id>/vote/\" to views.vote (name=\"vote\").","source_span":"path(\"<int:question_id>/\", views.detail, name=\"detail\"),"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial03","statement":"When a request comes for \"/polls/34/\", Django strips off \"polls/\" and passes \"34/\" to the polls.urls URLconf, resulting in a call to detail(request=<HttpRequest object>, question_id=34).","source_span":"resulting in a call to the ``detail()`` view"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial03","statement":"In a URL converter like \"<int:question_id>\", the colon separates the converter type from the pattern name.","source_span":"The colon (``:``) separates the converter and pattern name."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial03","statement":"The rewritten index() view selects the latest 5 poll questions ordered by \"-pub_date\" and joins their text with commas.","source_span":"latest_question_list = Question.objects.order_by(\"-pub_date\")[:5]"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial03","statement":"Every Django view must return an HttpResponse object containing the content for the requested page, or raise an exception such as Http404.","source_span":"returning an\n:class:`~django.http.HttpResponse` object containing the content for the\nrequested page, or raising an exception such as :exc:`~django.http.Http404`"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial03","statement":"Templates for the polls app should be placed in a \"templates\" directory created inside the polls directory.","source_span":"create a directory called ``templates`` in your ``polls`` directory"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial03","statement":"The default TEMPLATES setting configures a DjangoTemplates backend with APP_DIRS set to True, so Django looks for a \"templates\" subdirectory in each INSTALLED_APPS app.","source_span":"configures a ``DjangoTemplates``\nbackend whose :setting:`APP_DIRS <TEMPLATES-APP_DIRS>` option is set to\n``True``"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial03","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What four views does the poll application have according to Tutorial 3?","answer":"A question \"index\" page, a question \"detail\" page, a question \"results\" page, and a vote action."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial03","fact_refs":[3],"question":"When Django receives a request for \"/polls/34/\", what function call does it ultimately make?","answer":"Django strips off \"polls/\" and passes \"34/\" to the polls.urls URLconf, which results in a call to detail(request=<HttpRequest object>, question_id=34)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial03","fact_refs":[4],"question":"In the URL pattern \"<int:question_id>/\", what does the colon separate?","answer":"It separates the converter (int) from the pattern name (question_id)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial03","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How does the rewritten index() view decide which questions to display?","answer":"It orders Question objects by \"-pub_date\" and takes the latest 5, joining their text with commas."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial03","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What must every Django view function return or raise?","answer":"It must return an HttpResponse object containing the page's content, or raise an exception such as Http404."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial03","fact_refs":[7,8],"question":"Where should templates for the polls app be placed, and why does Django find them there automatically?","answer":"In a \"templates\" directory created inside the polls directory; Django finds them automatically because the default TEMPLATES setting has APP_DIRS set to True, making DjangoTemplates look in each INSTALLED_APPS app's \"templates\" subdirectory."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial04","statement":"The poll detail template's voting form uses method=\"post\" and includes the {% csrf_token %} template tag.","source_span":"<form action=\"{% url 'polls:vote' question.id %}\" method=\"post\">\n    {% csrf_token %}"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial04","statement":"Each radio button in the voting form has name=\"choice\" and a value equal to the associated choice's ID.","source_span":"The ``name`` of each radio button is ``\"choice\"``."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial04","statement":"Using method=\"post\" is important because submitting the form alters data server-side.","source_span":"Using ``method=\"post\"`` ... is very important, because the act of submitting this\n  form will alter data server-side"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial04","statement":"The vote() view retrieves the Question object using get_object_or_404(Question, pk=question_id).","source_span":"question = get_object_or_404(Question, pk=question_id)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial04","statement":"request.POST[\"choice\"] returns the ID of the selected choice as a string.","source_span":"``request.POST['choice']`` returns the ID of the selected choice, as a\n  string"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial04","statement":"If \"choice\" is missing from POST data, a KeyError is raised and the vote() view redisplays polls/detail.html with the error message \"You didn't select a choice.\"","source_span":"\"error_message\": \"You didn't select a choice.\","}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial04","statement":"The expression F(\"votes\") + 1 instructs the database to increase the vote count by 1.","source_span":"``F(\"votes\") + 1`` :ref:`instructs the database\n  <avoiding-race-conditions-using-f>` to increase the vote count by 1"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial04","statement":"After successfully recording a vote, the view returns HttpResponseRedirect(reverse(\"polls:results\", args=(question.id,))).","source_span":"return HttpResponseRedirect(reverse(\"polls:results\", args=(question.id,)))"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial04","statement":"Best practice is to always return an HttpResponseRedirect after successfully processing POST data, to prevent the data from being posted twice if a user hits the Back button.","source_span":"you should always return an\n  :class:`~django.http.HttpResponseRedirect` after successfully dealing with\n  POST data"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial04","statement":"In this example, the reverse() call returns a string like \"/polls/3/results/\", where 3 is the value of question.id.","source_span":"\"/polls/3/results/\""}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial04","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What two things does the poll voting form in detail.html include to safely submit data via POST?","answer":"It uses method=\"post\" and includes the {% csrf_token %} template tag."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial04","fact_refs":[3,5],"question":"How does the vote() view retrieve the Question object, and what happens if no choice was selected?","answer":"It retrieves the Question using get_object_or_404(Question, pk=question_id). If \"choice\" is missing from POST data, a KeyError is raised and the view redisplays polls/detail.html with the error message \"You didn't select a choice.\""}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial04","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What does the expression F(\"votes\") + 1 do in the vote() view?","answer":"It instructs the database to increase the vote count by 1."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial04","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What does the vote() view return after successfully recording a vote?","answer":"HttpResponseRedirect(reverse(\"polls:results\", args=(question.id,))), which redirects to the results page for that question."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial04","fact_refs":[8],"question":"Why should a view always return an HttpResponseRedirect after successfully processing POST data?","answer":"To prevent data from being posted twice if a user hits the Back button."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial04","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What type of value does request.POST[\"choice\"] return in the vote() view?","answer":"It returns the ID of the selected choice as a string."}
